QUENNE HYBRID INTELLIGENT STAR SHIP (Q-HISS)

Technical Specification — Version 1.0

QUENNE Deep-Space Cognitive Platform Architecture

Document Classification: Technical Concept Specification
Version: 1.0
Program: QUENNE Hybrid Intelligent Star Ship (Q-HISS)
Purpose: Define baseline system architecture and subsystem requirements for a long-duration intelligent deep-space platform.

⸻

1. System Overview

Q-HISS is a hybrid intelligent deep-space platform designed for autonomous and human-governed missions beyond Earth orbit.

The platform integrates:

• Hybrid photonic-electronic AI
• Neuromorphic reflex cognition
• Quantum-assisted navigation and planning
• Autonomous system management
• Human governance control

Primary mission domain:

• Lunar, Mars, and deep-space logistics
• Exploration and infrastructure deployment
• Long-duration crew or autonomous missions

⸻

2. Design Philosophy

The architecture follows QUENNE principles:
	1.	Intelligence is layered, not monolithic.
	2.	Machine-speed response with human authority preserved.
	3.	Systems must self-diagnose and adapt.
	4.	Energy and thermal intelligence are mission-critical.
	5.	Autonomy must remain governed and auditable.

⸻

3. High-Level System Architecture

Cognitive Stack
Sensors → Photonic Processing → Neuromorphic Reflex
         → Mission Intelligence → Governance Layer
         → Human Authority → Vehicle Control


Subsystem domains:
	1.	Navigation & sensing
	2.	Hybrid intelligence core
	3.	Autonomous systems management
	4.	Energy & thermal intelligence
	5.	Radiation & resilience layer
	6.	Human governance interface

⸻

4. Subsystem Specifications

4.1 Hybrid Intelligence Core

Purpose: Central cognitive engine.

Architecture:

• Photonic AI accelerator for high-speed perception
• Neuromorphic processors for reflex decisions
• Quantum-assisted mission optimization
• Electronic AI processors for general reasoning

Functions:

• Sensor fusion
• Threat & anomaly detection
• Mission optimization
• Adaptive planning

⸻

4.2 Navigation & Guidance System

Capabilities:

• Celestial navigation
• Quantum inertial navigation
• Star tracker integration
• Optical navigation support

Requirements:

• Operation without GPS
• Autonomous correction capability
• Multi-year drift stability target

⸻

4.3 Autonomous Systems Layer

Purpose: Maintain platform without constant human control.

Functions:

• Fault detection
• System reconfiguration
• Maintenance planning
• Damage response coordination

Examples:

• Power rerouting
• Module isolation
• Component health monitoring

⸻

4.4 Energy & Thermal Intelligence

Critical for deep-space operation.

Capabilities:

• Intelligent power allocation
• Thermal load prediction
• Heat rejection optimization
• Energy consumption balancing

Energy sources may include:

• Advanced solar arrays
• Nuclear or hybrid energy systems
• Long-duration battery storage

⸻

4.5 Radiation & Environmental Adaptation

Deep-space environment requires resilience.

Functions:

• Radiation exposure monitoring
• Component hardening strategies
• AI-driven exposure minimization
• Operational adaptation during storms

⸻

4.6 Human Governance Layer

Human authority remains mandatory.

Capabilities:

• Crew override authority
• Earth command integration
• Secure command authentication
• Decision audit logging

Autonomous operation remains bounded by governance constraints.

